<h2>How the Print Resolution Calculator works</h2><p>Printed image resolution is measured in pixels per inch. With resampling turned off, print width equals pixel width ÷ PPI and print height equals pixel height ÷ PPI. This tool also works in reverse for a chosen print width by calculating the effective PPI from the existing pixel dimensions.</p><h2>How to use this print resolution calculator</h2><p>Enter the image’s native pixel width and height, then a target PPI such as 300 or the value recommended by your print lab. The maximum-size result shows the physical dimensions that use those pixels without resampling. Enter a desired width to see the matching height and effective resolution for that print size.</p><h2>How to interpret the result</h2><p>PPI is not the same as a printer’s hardware DPI. Adobe’s print guidance treats image resolution and printer resolution as related but distinct concepts. A lower PPI can still look excellent at a larger viewing distance, while a close-viewed fine-art print may benefit from more source detail and careful sharpening.</p><h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2><p>The calculator does not judge image quality, focus, noise, demosaicing, interpolation or sharpening. Cropping reduces available pixels, and borderless printing may enlarge/crop slightly. Some labs resample automatically. Always confirm a provider’s preferred pixel dimensions, color space and sharpening workflow for critical output.</p><h2>Practical example and workflow</h2><p>A 6000 × 4000 image printed at 300 PPI is 20 × 13.33 inches without resampling. If you want a 30-inch-wide print from the same file, effective resolution becomes 200 PPI. That comparison helps you decide whether the larger print is acceptable for its intended viewing distance.</p>
